---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Showcase from "../../../components/Showcase.astro";

const install = `pnpm add @skryensya/core @skryensya/react`;

const titleHtml = `<header class="sk-stack" data-gap="none">
  <p class="sk-text" data-role="eyebrow">Integration</p>
  <h2 class="sk-heading" data-size="h3">Export settings</h2>
  <p class="sk-text" data-role="subtitle">
    Copy the derived CSS and paste it once into your project.
  </p>
</header>`;

const titleReact = `import { Text } from "@skryensya/react/text";
import { Heading } from "@skryensya/react/heading";

<header className="sk-stack" data-gap="none">
  <Text data-role="eyebrow">Integration</Text>
  <Heading as="h2" size="h3">Export settings</Heading>
  <Text data-role="subtitle">
    Copy the derived CSS and paste it once into your project.
  </Text>
</header>;`;

const readingHtml = `<article class="sk-stack" data-gap="sm">
  <span class="sk-text" data-role="eyebrow">
    Update · 5 minutes ago
  </span>
  <p class="sk-text" data-size="body" data-tone="primary">
    The deployment ended without interruptions for the people who
    They were already using the product.
  </p>
  <p class="sk-text" data-size="sm" data-tone="secondary">
    Version 2.18.4 is now available in Frankfurt and São Paulo.
  </p>
</article>`;

const readingReact = `import { Text } from "@skryensya/react/text";

<article className="sk-stack" data-gap="sm">
  <Text as="span" data-role="eyebrow">
    Update · 5 minutes ago
  </Text>
  <Text>
    The deployment ended without interruptions for the people who
    They were already using the product.
  </Text>
  <Text size="sm" tone="secondary">
    Version 2.18.4 is now available in Frankfurt and São Paulo.
  </Text>
</article>;`;

const inlineHtml = `<p class="sk-text">
  The <strong>Pro</strong> plan includes five active environments and
  preserves the reading flow within the same paragraph.
</p>`;

const inlineReact = `import { Text } from "@skryensya/react/text";

<Text>
  The <strong>Pro</strong> plan includes five active environments and
  preserves the reading flow within the same paragraph.
</Text>;`;

const feedbackHtml = `<p
  class="sk-text"
  data-size="sm"
  data-tone="danger"
  role="alert"
>
  The change could not be saved. Check the connection and try again.
</p>`;

const feedbackReact = `import { Text } from "@skryensya/react/text";

<Text role="alert" size="sm" tone="danger">
  The change could not be saved. Check the connection and try again.
</Text>;`;
---

<Base title="Text" description="Text: Reading text with typographic roles and explicit HTML semantics.">
  <h1>Text</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Text presents reading content with named typographic roles. Choose the HTML element that
    describes the content; Text does not create header semantics.
  </p>

  <h2>Title block</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Eyebrow, title and subtitle as a single unit.</strong> Two roles make up the header:
    <code>eyebrow</code> is the uppercase surtitle, <code>subtitle</code> is the lowercase under the title.
    The air between roles is provided by the brother below (<code>padding-block-start</code> of the heading and the
    subtitle), so the block stack uses <code>data-gap="none"</code>.
  </p>
  <Showcase html={titleHtml} react={titleReact} label="Title block" />

  <h2>Reading with context</h2>
  <p>A metadata must accompany the reading without competing with the body; hierarchy comes from role, tone and weight, not arbitrary sizes. The supertitle above uses the <code>eyebrow</code> role.</p>
  <Showcase html={readingHtml} react={readingReact} label="Update text" />

  <h2>Emphasis within a sentence</h2>
  <p>
    Use the semantic <strong>tag</strong>, not a weighted span: <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> inside
    Text takes the system's <code>emphasis</code> weight (not the browser's heaviest bold), and at the same time
    It is announced by a screen reader. <code>&lt;em&gt;</code> is in italics. The axis
    <code>data-weight</code> is for the weight of an entire Text; this is to emphasize <em>within</em> of
    the phrase.
  </p>
  <Showcase html={inlineHtml} react={inlineReact} label="inline text" />

  <h2>Validation feedback</h2>
  <p>The <code>danger</code> tone communicates the problem; the live region role is still a decision of the context that Text uses.</p>
  <Showcase html={feedbackHtml} react={feedbackReact} label="Validation message" />

  <h2>Install</h2>
  <CodeBlock code={install} label="terminal" />


  <h2>React</h2>
  <p>The code is in the <strong>React</strong> tab of the preview.</p>

  <h2>Contrato</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>sk-text</code> provides the base reading style.</li>
    <li><code>data-size</code>: <code>caption</code>, <code>sm</code>, <code>body</code> o <code>lg</code>.</li>
    <li><code>data-tone</code>: <code>primary</code>, <code>secondary</code>, <code>tertiary</code> o <code>danger</code>.</li>
    <li><code>data-weight</code>: <code>body</code>, <code>emphasis</code> o <code>label</code>.</li>
    <li>
      <code>data-role</code>: <code>eyebrow</code> (surtitle in uppercase) or <code>subtitle</code>
      (downloaded under a title). A role composes several axes at once; in React it is passed as
      <code>data-role</code>.
    </li>
    <li>In React, <code>as</code> accepts <code>p</code>, <code>div</code>, or <code>span</code>; The default is <code>p</code>.</li>
    <li>For headings, use Heading and a <code>h1</code>–<code>h6</code> element, not Text.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
